        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How tall should a standing desk be for a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              About your standing elbow height in shoes plus the pad's deck height, typically 44 to 54 inches for adults. Our standing desk height page has the formula and a chart by user height. Choose a desk whose maximum is at least an inch above your figure.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need a dual-motor desk for a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not strictly, but dual-motor desks with three-stage legs usually reach higher and stay steadier at full height, which is where a walking desk spends its time. Shorter walkers on slim pads can do well with a budget single-motor desk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my standing desk wobble when I walk?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Walking puts a rhythmic sideways force into the desk through your arms, and the higher the desk, the more it sways. Walk slower, avoid leaning on the edge, use low monitor mounts rather than tall arms, level the feet and avoid castors.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How deep should a walking desk be?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              About 30 inches is ideal. On a pad you stand a little further back, and a deep top lets you keep the keyboard at the front edge with the monitor at a comfortable distance.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will a walking pad fit under any standing desk?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if it fits between the desk's feet. Walking pads are about 19 to 23 inches wide; leave a few inches either side. Wider desktops generally leave more clearance, but check the distance between the feet, which most listings do not state.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use a desk converter on a fixed desk with a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if the fixed desk is high enough for the converter to reach your walking height, which is rare. Converters also wobble more than full desks. A full standing desk is usually the better choice.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

